<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eugenios of Trebizond</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 300</span> · Also known as Saint Eugenios</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Eugenios was an early Christian martyr of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>, said to have been executed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> around 300, and the patron saint of the city. His church was its main shrine, and the emperors of Trebizond struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> aspers bearing his image. Tradition credited him with saving the city from a Seljuk siege in the 1220s; his church later became the Yeni Cuma mosque.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eugenios-of-trebizond.jpg" alt="Eugenios of Trebizond" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Authors of Menologion of Basil II (circa 985 AC, Constantinople), Byzantine manuscript illuminators[1]: Pantoleon wit... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aquila,_Valerian,_Eugene_and_Candidus_of_Trebizond_(Menologion_of_Basil_II).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
